00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:26.270
MEERA KANHOUWA: Sure.

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:27.290
Thank you, Sean.

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:30.300
Everybody, it's a pleasure to
be here with you this afternoon.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.300
My name is Meera Kanhouwa.

00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:34.610
I'm an emergency
medicine physician.

00:12:34.610 --> 00:12:38.270
And I've practiced on the front
lines of inner city emergency

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:40.370
departments from
almost 20 years.

00:12:40.370 --> 00:12:43.790
I have physically laid
hands on 80,000 patients,

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:48.470
not including those I treated in
a war zone while I was in Iraq.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:54.410
I've seen every aspect of the
opiate situation from point

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:59.570
of injury, acute injury,
to heroin overdoses,

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:02.900
to deaths in my
emergency department.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.550
And let me tell you what.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:08.510
There's nothing that's
more painful than trying

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:11.600
to tell somebody at 3:00 in
the morning who is begging,

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:16.100
begging and crying for
350 milligrams of Demerol

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:19.850
that they can't have it
because you know that they're

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:21.320
on the road to addiction.

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:25.490
And it's a very, very difficult
and complicated disease

00:13:25.490 --> 00:13:28.290
to treat from a
provider perspective.

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:32.310
So I'm going to speak
to today as a provider.

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:36.830
I left clinical medicine
after 18 years of practice

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:39.920
because of the
promise of technology

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.760
having an ability to
truly transform the way

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:45.770
we deliver care.

00:13:45.770 --> 00:13:49.580
And in an industry
where our data

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:53.810
is growing at the rate
of petabytes per year--

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:55.760
petabytes per year, right?

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:59.690
We on the front lines taking
care of patients are data poor.

00:13:59.690 --> 00:14:01.580
Imagine that irony.

00:14:01.580 --> 00:14:04.230
How do you solve that problem?

00:14:04.230 --> 00:14:06.920
So what I would really
love for technology

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:08.870
to do for me and
all of my colleagues

00:14:08.870 --> 00:14:11.180
that take care of patients
day in and day out,

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:12.920
is make us smarter.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:15.800
Help me take care of
my patients better.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:19.010
Help me do it more efficiently
and more effectively

00:14:19.010 --> 00:14:21.320
so that they don't
keep bouncing back

00:14:21.320 --> 00:14:23.150
to the emergency department.

00:14:23.150 --> 00:14:25.460
And in our nation's
emergency departments,

00:14:25.460 --> 00:14:30.200
when I started practicing, we
had under 100 million visits

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:31.320
a year.

00:14:31.320 --> 00:14:34.040
Currently in the nation's
emergency departments,

00:14:34.040 --> 00:14:36.110
the growth to
emergency departments

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:40.370
has increased unfettered
at the rate of 8% to 12%

00:14:40.370 --> 00:14:43.370
per year for the last 25 years.

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:44.330
Why is that?

00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:45.740
People don't have primary care.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:48.900
They have no way
to go to get care.

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:51.050
They can't be seen
on a routine basis.

00:14:51.050 --> 00:14:52.700
Virtual health isn't deployed.

00:14:52.700 --> 00:14:53.658
They don't have access.

00:14:53.658 --> 00:14:54.951
They don't have transportation.

00:14:54.951 --> 00:14:56.930
They don't have
money to get there.

00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:59.330
And we know that
when they do this,

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:01.880
morbidity and
mortality increases

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:04.850
in a manner that is documented
in peer-reviewed literature.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:07.590
And we'll share some of
that with you later today.

00:15:07.590 --> 00:15:15.970
So with that, I think the best
way of putting this opioid use

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:18.400
disorder into perspective
is in the time

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:21.240
that we are all sitting
in this room today,

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:24.950
four souls will die.

00:15:24.950 --> 00:15:27.950
Four people will die while
we're sitting here in this room.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.890
And that's really
tragic, and especially

00:15:30.890 --> 00:15:35.390
when these deaths
are 100% preventable.

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:38.780
So we wanted to
build a tool that

00:15:38.780 --> 00:15:43.610
was able to move the needle to
the left that really helped us

00:15:43.610 --> 00:15:47.690
identify those patients who were
at risk so that you could offer

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:50.990
them better treatments
and better interventions.

00:15:50.990 --> 00:15:53.918
And with that, I'm going to
turn it back over to you, Sean.

00:15:53.918 --> 00:15:54.710
SEAN CONLIN: Great.

00:15:54.710 --> 00:15:55.490
So thanks, Meera.

00:15:55.490 --> 00:16:00.070
So from a data science
community perspective,

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:01.960
why don't we start
unpacking what

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:04.660
are the kinds of questions,
what kinds of predictions,

00:16:04.660 --> 00:16:09.653
what would be helpful to you
in the front response community

00:16:09.653 --> 00:16:10.570
that would be helpful?

00:16:10.570 --> 00:16:11.140
MEERA KANHOUWA: Yeah.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:11.640
Sure.

00:16:11.640 --> 00:16:14.860
So imagine you're in an
emergency department--

00:16:14.860 --> 00:16:17.590
because easy for me to do--

00:16:17.590 --> 00:16:20.590
and you get two people who
both come in with back pain.

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:23.980
Both of them have been
prescribed T3s, Percocet,

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:28.210
Ativan, Flexeril, whatever the
cocktail du jour is that they

00:16:28.210 --> 00:16:30.370
got, and they're both there.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:32.980
It's impossible for
me to know who's

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:35.650
going to be at higher
risk of addiction,

00:16:35.650 --> 00:16:38.990
because I'm treating acute
pain at a point in time.

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:41.800
But that's also the best
time for me to intervene.

00:16:41.800 --> 00:16:45.460
It may be the only time
they see medical care.

00:16:45.460 --> 00:16:48.730
So if we can figure out
who is at higher risk,

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:51.220
I could then pull
my case managers in.

00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:53.470
I could get them to follow up.

00:16:53.470 --> 00:16:56.980
I could do whatever I had to
do to get systems in place

00:16:56.980 --> 00:16:57.900
to get them in care.

00:16:57.900 --> 00:16:59.400
So that would be
the first question.

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:02.020
SEAN CONLIN: All right, so we
guessed the first one right.

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:02.830
Who is at risk?

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:03.330
OK?

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:04.990
MEERA KANHOUWA: Yeah.

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:08.650
And I think the next question
is-- can we turn down

00:17:08.650 --> 00:17:09.940
the volume on me a little bit?

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:10.359
I'm sorry.

00:17:10.359 --> 00:17:11.560
I'm hearing a lot of reverb.

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:15.230
Thank you-- is really
understanding what

00:17:15.230 --> 00:17:16.480
the barriers to treatment are.

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:20.230
So medical treatment for
opiate addiction does work.

00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:21.400
It is very effective.

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:23.170
There is some recidivism.

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:26.710
And that is because when
you are addicted to opiates,

00:17:26.710 --> 00:17:30.220
you really have changes going
on in your neuronal pathways

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:31.600
in your brain.

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:35.240
So there is a rewiring of
your brain that does occur.

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:39.520
So the medication
treatment protocols

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.560
have to actually bring you back.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:46.630
And only about two
people out of 10

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:49.510
that actually would benefit
from medical treatment

00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:51.490
are actually
receiving that today.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:55.000
So it would be great to know
what the barriers to people

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:56.485
getting that treatment are.

00:17:56.485 --> 00:17:59.440
SEAN CONLIN: So we guessed
right on the second one too.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:02.180
But let's just emphasize
that for a minute.

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:07.390
So nationally, the
scientific community

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:08.870
has done an amazing job.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:10.903
We have medical
treatment that can

00:18:10.903 --> 00:18:12.070
help those who are addicted.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:12.915
MEERA KANHOUWA:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:18:12.915 --> 00:18:14.082
SEAN CONLIN: Very effective.

00:18:14.082 --> 00:18:15.130
The evidence is in.

00:18:15.130 --> 00:18:15.940
MEERA KANHOUWA:
The evidence is in.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:17.648
SEAN CONLIN: That's
probably the only ray

00:18:17.648 --> 00:18:19.390
of sunshine in this whole mess.

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:21.070
But as a nation--

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:23.290
think about this--
of all of those

00:18:23.290 --> 00:18:26.230
who need to be on medical
treatment, and it works,

00:18:26.230 --> 00:18:30.960
only two out of 10 individuals
are getting access to that.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.180
And as a nation, what
are those barriers

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:36.180
keeping people from getting
access to that treatment?

00:18:36.180 --> 00:18:39.000
The country is spending
a fortune on treatments,

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:40.800
but people are
not getting there.

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:43.860
And unpacking that and
understanding what that is

00:18:43.860 --> 00:18:46.620
and how AI and
analytics and data

00:18:46.620 --> 00:18:48.690
can help the decision
makers better

00:18:48.690 --> 00:18:51.585
manage that is an important
issue for the nation.

00:18:51.585 --> 00:18:52.710
MEERA KANHOUWA: Absolutely.

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:53.790
Absolutely.

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:57.030
And I think to better
understand this,

00:18:57.030 --> 00:19:01.090
it would really be good to know
what kinds of interventions?

00:19:01.090 --> 00:19:04.050
Now typically, when you hear
a doctor say intervention,

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:08.820
you're thinking oh some kind
of procedure, a doctor thing.

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:10.320
But not really, right?

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:16.290
We do know at this point that
social determinants of health

00:19:16.290 --> 00:19:21.180
are 80% of the equation when
it comes to our own health

00:19:21.180 --> 00:19:23.160
and determining
how healthy we are.

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:25.080
And when you think about
social determinants,

00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:30.090
think about getting good
sleep, exercise, diet,

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:33.100
and how you live,
where you live,

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:35.250
who you are surrounded by.

00:19:35.250 --> 00:19:37.280
These are all social
determinants of health.

00:19:37.280 --> 00:19:40.770
So understanding what
the interventions are,

00:19:40.770 --> 00:19:43.650
we're actually talking about
human services interventions,

00:19:43.650 --> 00:19:45.550
not medical treatments.

00:19:45.550 --> 00:19:47.737
So I look at those
as different things.

00:19:47.737 --> 00:19:50.070
SEAN CONLIN: So let me just
say back when I heard there.

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:52.230
So there's medical treatment.

00:19:52.230 --> 00:19:56.100
And that's not what we're
talking about with Opioid360.

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:59.670
But a very big influence
around whether somebody

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.280
gets healthy or not,
or is successful

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:04.320
against the addiction,
is whether or not

00:20:04.320 --> 00:20:06.432
they have the right social
services around them?

00:20:06.432 --> 00:20:07.890
MEERA KANHOUWA:
Social determinants

00:20:07.890 --> 00:20:09.230
of health, absolutely.

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:13.290
So there is increasing
peer-reviewed literature

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:17.460
in many of the leading
journals about how

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:21.870
those social determinants
actually improve or decrease

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:22.440
our health.

00:20:22.440 --> 00:20:23.430
SEAN CONLIN: OK.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:26.670
So from a data science
community perspective

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:29.400
to those on the front
line, if we can help

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:32.550
you better understand early
in the lifecycle who's

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:35.460
at elevated risk of
opiate use disorder,

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:38.910
if we can help you identify
at the individual level

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:41.550
what the barriers
to treatment are,

00:20:41.550 --> 00:20:45.210
and then if we can help
make suggestions of what

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:48.270
are optimal interventions
for individuals,

00:20:48.270 --> 00:20:49.358
that would be helpful.

00:20:49.358 --> 00:20:51.150
MEERA KANHOUWA: It
would be really helpful.

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:53.320
If you could move the
needle to the left

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:57.120
and get those people who
are at highest risk--

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:59.460
finding that needle
in a haystack,

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:02.640
and get them into care
programs before they

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:04.620
get addicted to the
point where they end up

00:21:04.620 --> 00:21:06.680
seeing me from their
heroin overdose, that

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:07.740
would be really great.

00:21:07.740 --> 00:21:08.698
SEAN CONLIN: Excellent.

00:21:08.698 --> 00:21:14.270
OK, so let's unpack Opiod360
and talk about first the data.

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:16.530
Before we get into the
details of the data,

00:21:16.530 --> 00:21:18.030
let's level set on this.

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:21.780
First and foremost, personal
identified information

00:21:21.780 --> 00:21:25.050
is protected here.

00:21:25.050 --> 00:21:27.030
We de-identified
the data and then

00:21:27.030 --> 00:21:32.430
we bring it together
in Opioid360.

00:21:32.430 --> 00:21:35.220
There are three
major areas of data

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:36.900
that are fed into this system.

00:21:36.900 --> 00:21:39.600
And this is very
large data sets.

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:42.150
The primary users of it
currently and probably

00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:44.900
for the foreseeable
near term future

00:21:44.900 --> 00:21:48.068
are governments, state
governments, in particular.

00:21:48.068 --> 00:21:49.860
And if you think about
a state government--

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:52.560
this is the first data set,
the public sector data--

00:21:52.560 --> 00:21:55.950
you've got three major areas
of the state government

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:58.320
that are dealing with
this opiate crisis.

00:21:58.320 --> 00:22:00.300
You've got the health,
the human services,

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:03.340
and the law enforcement parts
of the state government.

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:06.190
And if you peel that back,
that's 25 or 30 different data

00:22:06.190 --> 00:22:09.990
sets typically, if you
go from state to state.

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:12.590
And being able to bring
that data together,

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:14.430
de-identified--
because keep in mind,

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:17.190
you have CJIS laws and
HIPAA laws, and all sorts

00:22:17.190 --> 00:22:19.680
of complex legal requirements.

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:22.267
So you de-identify the
data where it sits.

00:22:22.267 --> 00:22:23.850
And then you bring
it together and you

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:26.550
can link the de-identified
data together.

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:30.390
And the purpose of Opioid360
is not at the individual level

00:22:30.390 --> 00:22:34.590
per se, but it's more to be able
to look at millions of examples

00:22:34.590 --> 00:22:39.120
over 10, 20 years, and look
for patterns in those data

00:22:39.120 --> 00:22:43.470
sets that are strongly
indicative of those three

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:47.310
questions-- elevated
risk, and what's worked,

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:50.310
what interventions have
been successful for people

00:22:50.310 --> 00:22:53.100
who fit into that persona,
and which ones have not

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:55.830
been-- so you can more
intelligently make decisions

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:57.780
around resource allocation.

00:22:57.780 --> 00:23:00.670
That's the first of the
three kinds of data.

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:05.310
The second one is what's
called a lifestyle data set.

00:23:05.310 --> 00:23:07.810
Meera talked about social
determinants of health.

00:23:07.810 --> 00:23:10.560
So there is publicly
accessible data

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:14.830
that gives indications of
particular things on lifestyle.

00:23:14.830 --> 00:23:16.540
And we'll look at
this in just a minute.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.450
But that's an
important data set.

00:23:18.450 --> 00:23:21.000
And then there's a third
one through our partnership

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:24.170
with Google, a very
important data set

00:23:24.170 --> 00:23:27.230
that's from Google Analytics.

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:29.130
And there's some very
interesting research.

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:31.930
One of the most recent ones
was in a "Scientific American"

00:23:31.930 --> 00:23:36.430
article from December that
shows a positive correlation

00:23:36.430 --> 00:23:40.720
between certain
Google data being

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:44.890
an early indicator of what
becomes opioid use or even

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:46.540
opioid-related death.

00:23:46.540 --> 00:23:50.050
And it's through the analytics
engine we aggregated up

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:52.420
to a geography footprint.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:57.670
PII is strictly protected, just
as any of the other data sets.

00:23:57.670 --> 00:24:02.720
So that's the data
that we bring in.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:06.350
One thing I'd emphasize too--
and then I'll pull you back in,

00:24:06.350 --> 00:24:10.620
Meera-- is we crafted
this data very carefully.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:12.230
One of the biggest
problems that we've

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:14.390
heard from those
on the front line

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:18.315
is that the data tends to be
very old, relatively speaking.

00:24:18.315 --> 00:24:21.170
MEERA KANHOUWA: Yeah, so when
you're looking at public sector

00:24:21.170 --> 00:24:25.640
data, typically claims data
is going to be aged anywhere

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:29.190
from 60 to 90 days or more.

00:24:29.190 --> 00:24:32.130
ED visit data for
those registries,

00:24:32.130 --> 00:24:34.160
you can think about
aging of the data

00:24:34.160 --> 00:24:37.250
all the way up to
about 180 days.

00:24:37.250 --> 00:24:40.580
PDMPs, or Prescription
Drug Monitoring Programs,

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:44.075
could have anywhere from a
30 to 90 day lag as well.

00:24:44.075 --> 00:24:48.230
So as you're trying to identify,
and predictably identify who

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:53.090
these patients are, what we
really need is real-time data,

00:24:53.090 --> 00:24:54.950
something that would--

00:24:54.950 --> 00:24:58.100
either leveraging the
local health data exchange

00:24:58.100 --> 00:24:59.450
for the medical piece.

00:24:59.450 --> 00:25:03.290
But again, keep in mind,
the medical data is only 20%

00:25:03.290 --> 00:25:04.250
of the story.

00:25:04.250 --> 00:25:06.440
The social determinants
and the lifestyle data

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:09.290
you talked about, that's
the 80% of the story

00:25:09.290 --> 00:25:12.200
that I hope you're going to
tell us moves the needle.

00:25:12.200 --> 00:25:13.130
SEAN CONLIN: Exactly.

00:25:13.130 --> 00:25:17.790
So the Google data is
the most current data.

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:20.040
The lifestyle data
is updated monthly.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.298
So that's a 0 to 30 data set.

00:25:22.298 --> 00:25:23.840
And then you've got
the public sector

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:25.790
data that is after 30 days.

00:25:25.790 --> 00:25:30.950
So now you have a complete
continuum of timely data

00:25:30.950 --> 00:25:33.470
that the community just
doesn't have today.

00:25:33.470 --> 00:25:35.720
So that harnessed
and brought together

00:25:35.720 --> 00:25:39.900
in an integrated fashion,
is very important.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:42.150
Maybe an example
would be helpful.

00:25:42.150 --> 00:25:44.040
So imagine that we'd
looked at millions

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:47.200
of these cases over
many, many years,

00:25:47.200 --> 00:25:50.610
and we had extracted
clusters or personas that

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:54.930
were indicative of groups
that were relatively safe,

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:59.582
and ones that are very much at
risk of developing addiction.

00:26:03.198 --> 00:26:04.910
And we're going to
compare two groups.

00:26:09.833 --> 00:26:12.000
And we're going to start
with just the medical data.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:13.780
What Meera was talking about--

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:16.350
and this is the data that
our front line practitioners

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:17.710
typically deal with today.

00:26:17.710 --> 00:26:21.000
This is what they
were left with.

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:23.730
In each of these
populations, you've

00:26:23.730 --> 00:26:26.690
got individuals who
had a back pain.

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:30.500
They had been in rehab
for three to six months.

00:26:30.500 --> 00:26:32.950
They were put onto an
opiate prescription.

00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:35.050
And the question is
what's the likelihood

00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:38.110
that an individual in
either of those personas

00:26:38.110 --> 00:26:39.435
would develop an addiction?

00:26:39.435 --> 00:26:40.810
We're not talking
the individual,

00:26:40.810 --> 00:26:42.880
but what is the tendency?

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:48.460
A good analogy is when we buy on
Amazon, they make suggestions.

00:26:48.460 --> 00:26:51.790
They say, if you buy this,
you might want to buy this.

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:53.710
If you're in one
of those personas,

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:57.040
it's not deterministic,
but there's an indication

00:26:57.040 --> 00:27:00.040
that you may be at much
higher risk than somebody else

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:01.540
that you would
develop an addiction.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:02.415
MEERA KANHOUWA: Yeah.

00:27:02.415 --> 00:27:05.350
And Sean, this is actually what
I was talking about before.

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:07.378
When you've got two
patients who show up,

00:27:07.378 --> 00:27:08.920
they both have low
back pain, they've

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:11.860
both been on similar
meds, you have no way

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:13.840
of knowing at the
point of care who's

00:27:13.840 --> 00:27:16.750
at higher risk of addiction,
even if one of them

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.480
took an ambulance to come see
you at 3:00 in the morning

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:21.940
because their back is spasming.

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:24.920
I mean, we just have
no way of knowing.

00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:27.402
So why don't you show us with
this lifestyle data does.

00:27:27.402 --> 00:27:28.360
SEAN CONLIN: All right.

00:27:28.360 --> 00:27:31.740
So let's take a closer
look at persona A. We're

00:27:31.740 --> 00:27:33.760
going to pull in lifestyle.

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:38.935
And we're going to pull
in financial information.

00:27:38.935 --> 00:27:40.518
MEERA KANHOUWA: So
you know, financial

00:27:40.518 --> 00:27:41.440
is pretty interesting.

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:45.180
I mean, it turns out that 1/3 of
all bankruptcies in the United

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:48.510
States are due to medical costs.

00:27:48.510 --> 00:27:51.570
And there is an
absolute correlation

00:27:51.570 --> 00:27:56.460
between your overall health
and your financial status.

00:27:56.460 --> 00:27:59.550
And not only that,
there is now starting

00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:04.380
to become a socioeconomic
divide for those people

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:08.910
in certain populations
whose lifespan has actually

00:28:08.910 --> 00:28:11.850
decreased.

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:14.150
In the United
States our lifespans

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:17.220
are decreasing based on
your socioeconomic status.

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:20.190
Now that's appalling
in this nation.

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:23.040
We are the richest
nation on earth,

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:27.780
and we have never, ever been
in the top 10 OECD countries

00:28:27.780 --> 00:28:28.980
in terms of delivering care.

00:28:28.980 --> 00:28:30.990
We've never even made
it to the top 20.

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:33.515
Frankly, we never
made it to the top 30.

00:28:33.515 --> 00:28:35.890
When I started looking at the
data submitted by the World

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:39.450
Health Organization, we were
about number-- in the 80s.

00:28:39.450 --> 00:28:41.250
Now we're somewhere in the 40s.

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:44.490
So as you start
understanding how

00:28:44.490 --> 00:28:48.463
the financial aspects really
impact overall health,

00:28:48.463 --> 00:28:49.380
I think it's critical.

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:52.220
People don't look at this
and they don't know about it.

00:28:52.220 --> 00:28:53.460
SEAN CONLIN: Thank you.

00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:59.540
So people in persona
A tend to be married.

00:28:59.540 --> 00:29:00.860
They tend to have kids.

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:02.870
They tend to own their home.

00:29:02.870 --> 00:29:05.270
They tend to eat healthy.

00:29:05.270 --> 00:29:09.260
They start exercise pretty
quickly after they've--

00:29:09.260 --> 00:29:12.800
for example, into this scenario,
they'd be back exercising.

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:14.350
They have short commutes.

00:29:14.350 --> 00:29:16.370
They have strong
financial indicators,

00:29:16.370 --> 00:29:17.840
good financial stability.

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:22.830
And they live in
economically growing areas.

00:29:22.830 --> 00:29:26.830
And if you look at
the neighborhood

00:29:26.830 --> 00:29:30.280
or the geographic
area where they are,

00:29:30.280 --> 00:29:35.427
they tend to be in areas where
the Google data indicates

00:29:35.427 --> 00:29:37.510
that there isn't a lot of
activity that correlates

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:41.650
to high risk opiate abuse.

00:29:41.650 --> 00:29:46.660
Now if we look at persona B,
it's a very different picture.

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:50.530
People in this persona tend to,
again, it's not deterministic,

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:54.760
but they tend to have
a high rate of divorce.

00:29:54.760 --> 00:29:59.560
They tend to have unhealthy
eating habits, long commutes.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:01.630
They don't exercise.

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:04.180
There's a lot of bankruptcy
and financial distress

00:30:04.180 --> 00:30:06.640
in this area.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:09.580
And they tend to live
in areas that are not

00:30:09.580 --> 00:30:12.490
economically stable or growing.

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:15.130
And if you look at the
geographic footprint

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:16.780
from the Google data--

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:18.170
you can't go to PII--

00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:20.290
but in that general
area, it tends

00:30:20.290 --> 00:30:24.190
to have a high activity
around high risk search terms

00:30:24.190 --> 00:30:27.850
that correlate with
opioid use disorder.

00:30:32.020 --> 00:30:34.210
The predictive models,
when we started here,

00:30:34.210 --> 00:30:35.980
based just on the
health data, they

00:30:35.980 --> 00:30:38.710
predicted 10% to 15% chance.

00:30:38.710 --> 00:30:41.300
And they were both about
the same prediction.

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:45.870
Now with this new
data, we revisit it.

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:48.600
Persona A actually
dropped significantly.

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:53.220
And persona B, individuals in
that group, went up fourfold.

00:30:53.220 --> 00:30:54.330
Massive increase.

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:56.940
And this actually
shows up in the data.

00:30:56.940 --> 00:30:59.560
It very much
correlates in the data.

00:30:59.560 --> 00:31:03.630
And the machine learning
predictive models show that.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:06.017
Let me just give you a very
brief anecdote, if I can.

00:31:06.017 --> 00:31:08.100
MEERA KANHOUWA: And I'd
like to share one as well.

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:10.050
SEAN CONLIN: Yeah,
why don't you share.

00:31:10.050 --> 00:31:12.975
And then I'll share a
war story with folks.

00:31:12.975 --> 00:31:15.290
MEERA KANHOUWA: We
love war stories.

00:31:15.290 --> 00:31:18.900
I think that providers
might ask you well,

00:31:18.900 --> 00:31:23.820
how is Google data going to
help me be a better doctor?

00:31:23.820 --> 00:31:27.370
And my answer to them, quite
frankly, is the following.

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:29.130
As you look at these
lifestyle data--

00:31:29.130 --> 00:31:32.550
this is based on
millions of people--

00:31:32.550 --> 00:31:38.100
as you look at drugs that are
going through clinical trial,

00:31:38.100 --> 00:31:41.940
you're lucky to get a sample
size of 10,000 patients.

00:31:41.940 --> 00:31:43.890
Like, that's an awesome trial.

00:31:43.890 --> 00:31:44.580
All right?

00:31:44.580 --> 00:31:47.520
Most of these sample
sizes for FDA trials

00:31:47.520 --> 00:31:50.790
are 5,000, 8,000 patients.

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:53.320
This is millions of patients.

00:31:53.320 --> 00:31:56.790
So as we think about is
this data statistically

00:31:56.790 --> 00:31:58.920
significant--

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:01.835
I mean, we haven't done a
chi-square regression analysis

00:32:01.835 --> 00:32:03.210
to actually answer
that question.

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:07.110
But you could intuitively
gut understand

00:32:07.110 --> 00:32:09.990
that the sample size is probably
going to be very impactful.

00:32:09.990 --> 00:32:12.990
And I'm excited about
the kinds of studies

00:32:12.990 --> 00:32:15.270
that we could do
based on this data,

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:18.600
and showing how it really can
help us determine outcomes.

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:20.940
SEAN CONLIN: Excellent.

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:25.010
I'd like to draw your
attention to the predictions.

00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:27.640
So there's an
interesting set of work

00:32:27.640 --> 00:32:31.900
done for the state
in the Midwest,

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:33.910
for their Medicaid population.

00:32:33.910 --> 00:32:37.900
And we were given well
over a million records

00:32:37.900 --> 00:32:39.340
that went back over 10 years.

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:42.430
And the question was build
a predictive AI model

00:32:42.430 --> 00:32:45.100
that can identify within
that population who

00:32:45.100 --> 00:32:47.830
is at elevated risk of
opioid use disorder.

00:32:47.830 --> 00:32:49.240
That was the question.

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:52.490
So we built the
machine learning model.

00:32:52.490 --> 00:32:54.240
And when we brought
the results forward,

00:32:54.240 --> 00:32:56.920
the client shared with us
that they had actually built

00:32:56.920 --> 00:32:58.060
their own predictive model.

00:32:58.060 --> 00:32:59.560
And they wanted to
compare that two.

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:01.150
So we said, great,
let's compare them.

00:33:01.150 --> 00:33:02.990
They used a fundamentally
different approach.

00:33:02.990 --> 00:33:04.540
They had used a
rules-based approach.

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:07.390
It had hundreds of rules
that were from experts

00:33:07.390 --> 00:33:09.400
like Meera that
said things like,

00:33:09.400 --> 00:33:11.350
if somebody goes more
than 30 miles to get

00:33:11.350 --> 00:33:14.230
an opiate prescription
filled at a pharmacy, that's

00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:15.113
a risk factor.

00:33:15.113 --> 00:33:16.780
If they've got more
than one doctor that

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:18.490
gives them an
opiate prescription,

00:33:18.490 --> 00:33:19.660
that's a risk factor.

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:23.320
And they had hundreds of rules
they developed over years.

00:33:23.320 --> 00:33:24.850
And we used a machine
learning model

00:33:24.850 --> 00:33:26.058
that just looked at the data.

00:33:26.058 --> 00:33:30.100
But it had a massive data
set to go train off of.

00:33:30.100 --> 00:33:31.930
Their predictive
model identified

00:33:31.930 --> 00:33:35.050
15% of the target
population, those

00:33:35.050 --> 00:33:37.170
who are at elevated
risk of opiate abuse.

00:33:37.170 --> 00:33:40.150
Our are identified 85%.

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:41.860
They had a 50% error rate.

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:43.720
We had a 10% error rate.

00:33:43.720 --> 00:33:45.580
And probably most
importantly, theirs

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:47.080
took about nine
months of runtime

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:48.580
for a given individual
from the time

00:33:48.580 --> 00:33:51.260
they first started their
opioid prescription

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:54.070
until the system had enough
data to make a prediction.

00:33:54.070 --> 00:33:56.767
Ours could do it in four to
five months, half that time.

00:33:56.767 --> 00:33:59.100
And we've already got models
in the works that are going

00:33:59.100 --> 00:34:00.820
to pull that off much shorter.

00:34:00.820 --> 00:34:03.130
And early prediction,
early intervention,

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:06.207
early identification is
the key to success here.

00:34:06.207 --> 00:34:07.540
And it's showing up in the data.

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:08.795
It's showing up in the models.

00:34:08.795 --> 00:34:09.670
MEERA KANHOUWA: Yeah.

00:34:09.670 --> 00:34:14.650
Just for the audience,
this is really only showing

00:34:14.650 --> 00:34:18.370
those personas that are
impacted by medical prescription

00:34:18.370 --> 00:34:20.020
as their start to opiates.

00:34:20.020 --> 00:34:25.120
We know there's another persona
which is through illegal means,

00:34:25.120 --> 00:34:28.150
getting heroin, IV fentanyl,
stuff like that on the street.

00:34:28.150 --> 00:34:32.170
So that's a whole other
way to evaluate risk.

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:34.870
SEAN CONLIN: And we've
actually made progress on that.

00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:36.830
That is a difficult problem.

00:34:36.830 --> 00:34:38.889
But instead of one
clean area where

00:34:38.889 --> 00:34:40.719
you can trace it through
the prescriptions

00:34:40.719 --> 00:34:43.840
through the doctors, there
are about eight to nine

00:34:43.840 --> 00:34:46.330
different variables
that can be analyzed

00:34:46.330 --> 00:34:49.120
to identify those
at-risk populations,

00:34:49.120 --> 00:34:52.270
and basically be a surrogate
for the data that's

00:34:52.270 --> 00:34:53.830
in the clean
prescription data set.

00:34:53.830 --> 00:34:56.281
MEERA KANHOUWA: Great.

00:34:56.281 --> 00:34:59.200
SEAN CONLIN: So why
don't we take a look

00:34:59.200 --> 00:35:03.850
and share some specific
examples, some actual use

00:35:03.850 --> 00:35:05.476
cases.

00:35:05.476 --> 00:35:12.040
And Meera, this one is an
example of a statewide policy

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:13.270
executive.

00:35:13.270 --> 00:35:17.530
Think Medicaid policy
executive, who's

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:19.570
looking across an
entire state population.

00:35:19.570 --> 00:35:24.180
And to tee this up, because
we have this lifestyle data,

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:27.130
for demo or for
example purposes, what

00:35:27.130 --> 00:35:28.630
you're about to see
here didn't have

00:35:28.630 --> 00:35:30.430
the benefit of the state data.

00:35:30.430 --> 00:35:33.770
And it didn't have the benefit
of the Google Analytics data.

00:35:33.770 --> 00:35:35.570
There's just the lifestyle data.

00:35:35.570 --> 00:35:38.920
So what we did is we pulled the
data down on 7.7 million people

00:35:38.920 --> 00:35:41.640
who live in a southeast state.

00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:43.492
All the PII is stripped out.

00:35:43.492 --> 00:35:44.950
They're just random
numbers, you'll

00:35:44.950 --> 00:35:46.880
see that here in a minute.

00:35:46.880 --> 00:35:51.230
But we've got lifestyle
data on 7.7 million people.

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.050
So in this one screen,
you're interacting

00:35:53.050 --> 00:35:55.780
with that complexity.

00:35:55.780 --> 00:35:58.390
Then on top of that, we
apply predictive AI models

00:35:58.390 --> 00:35:59.840
in a series of them.

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:01.610
So if you go across
the top there,

00:36:01.610 --> 00:36:03.422
one of the things we said is--

00:36:03.422 --> 00:36:05.380
for every one of them,
we put a score on them--

00:36:05.380 --> 00:36:08.860
what's the likelihood of being
at elevated risk of opioid use

00:36:08.860 --> 00:36:09.430
disorder?

00:36:09.430 --> 00:36:12.347
And you'll see there
about 140,000 individuals.

00:36:12.347 --> 00:36:14.680
Kind of depends on where you
put the tear line of what's

00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:15.190
high risk.

00:36:15.190 --> 00:36:17.370
But it identified that.

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:19.780
There about 2.2 million
who are at high risk

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:22.010
of being uninsured.

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:24.700
This is not a Medicaid
expansion state.

00:36:24.700 --> 00:36:27.180
And the state confirmed that
that's probably about right.

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:28.930
When you integrate it
with the state data,

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:31.780
that number gets dialed
in quite tightly,

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:33.747
although they even
struggle with that number.

00:36:33.747 --> 00:36:35.080
And then on the left hand side--

00:36:35.080 --> 00:36:36.997
I'm not going to ask you
to actually read it--

00:36:36.997 --> 00:36:39.320
but there are a series of
filters that we can apply.

00:36:39.320 --> 00:36:42.833
So Meera, for example, one of
the high risk populations--

00:36:42.833 --> 00:36:45.250
and I know you had to deal
with it in the emergency room--

00:36:45.250 --> 00:36:51.340
is what if we could look across
7.7 million and identify based

00:36:51.340 --> 00:36:56.070
on generic individuals--
so the PII is protected--

00:36:56.070 --> 00:37:01.830
pregnant, likely at high
risk of opiate addiction,

00:37:01.830 --> 00:37:06.342
uninsured, housing insecurity,
and they have children at home.

00:37:06.342 --> 00:37:07.800
That's a very high
risk population.

00:37:07.800 --> 00:37:08.505
Any thoughts?

00:37:08.505 --> 00:37:12.390
MEERA KANHOUWA: Well,
it's even worse than that

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:15.420
because a pregnant mom
is going to give birth

00:37:15.420 --> 00:37:17.010
to an addicted baby.

00:37:17.010 --> 00:37:19.020
And that baby will be premature.

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:21.990
And that baby will
spend on average 45 days

00:37:21.990 --> 00:37:24.300
in the neonatal
intensive care unit.

00:37:24.300 --> 00:37:26.940
That baby will have
IVs and monitors,

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:28.710
will likely be intubated.

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:31.260
And that baby will go
through withdrawal.

00:37:31.260 --> 00:37:33.780
That tiny little
neonate will withdrawal

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:35.070
in front of your eyes.

00:37:35.070 --> 00:37:36.720
And it's terrible.

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:42.471
And these babies cost about
half a million dollars

00:37:42.471 --> 00:37:46.130
to make it through
that NICU alive.

00:37:46.130 --> 00:37:48.510
And they don't all
make it out alive.

00:37:48.510 --> 00:37:51.000
So this is a really
huge problem when

00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:56.400
we're talking about collateral
damage from opiate addiction.

00:37:56.400 --> 00:38:00.720
I mean, you know, these are
youngest, youngest souls coming

00:38:00.720 --> 00:38:01.630
into this world.

00:38:01.630 --> 00:38:05.220
And if you could move
the needle to the left

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:08.140
and not have that
pregnant mom get addicted,

00:38:08.140 --> 00:38:10.560
and not have her give
birth to an addicted baby,

00:38:10.560 --> 00:38:11.970
you'd actually be saving lives.

00:38:14.143 --> 00:38:16.060
SEAN CONLIN: I don't
know what to say to that.

00:38:16.060 --> 00:38:19.150
It's a good reminder
that this stuff matters.

00:38:19.150 --> 00:38:21.132
MEERA KANHOUWA: It does
matter, absolutely.

00:38:21.132 --> 00:38:22.840
SEAN CONLIN: We're
not just talking data.

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:25.370
We're talking lives.

00:38:25.370 --> 00:38:28.630
So what we just did
there, we actually

00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:30.880
applied all those filters.

00:38:30.880 --> 00:38:33.400
Depends where you put high risk.

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:37.450
Out of 100 scale, where
100 is extremely high risk

00:38:37.450 --> 00:38:40.410
of addiction, this
is 60 and above.

00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:42.880
And there 5,000
individuals in this state

00:38:42.880 --> 00:38:47.090
that were identified, fitting
what we just talked about.

00:38:47.090 --> 00:38:50.260
If you move it up to
90, so 90 out of 100,

00:38:50.260 --> 00:38:52.430
that number reduces
down to about 500.

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:54.090
But that's still 500.

00:38:54.090 --> 00:38:58.560
And by way, this is data
from the last 30 days.

00:38:58.560 --> 00:39:03.240
So that's 500 people
who fit that criteria,

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:08.160
that you can see by the map
which counties they live in,

00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:10.260
where the densities are.

00:39:10.260 --> 00:39:12.270
We also, in the
upper right corner,

00:39:12.270 --> 00:39:15.720
again, it's hard to see
in a room like this,

00:39:15.720 --> 00:39:17.910
but for each of
those individuals--

00:39:17.910 --> 00:39:21.030
again, the PII is protected--

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:25.710
we use data to take
a educated guess

00:39:25.710 --> 00:39:27.280
at what are the
barriers to treatment

00:39:27.280 --> 00:39:30.930
that individual is up against.

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:33.290
And then based on
that, we aggregate it.

00:39:33.290 --> 00:39:36.330
So we can look across the
500 or the 5,000 individuals

00:39:36.330 --> 00:39:39.540
and aggregate that up for
the policy decision maker.

00:39:39.540 --> 00:39:42.030
And they can say, if
I pull these policy

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:46.740
levers in these
particular geographies,

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:48.310
how many people can I help?

00:39:48.310 --> 00:39:50.040
And I've got limited resources.

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:53.070
And how do I get those out
there and get the most benefit

00:39:53.070 --> 00:39:55.740
for society and for
these individuals?

00:39:55.740 --> 00:39:58.980
And this is data and capability
and predictions they've never

00:39:58.980 --> 00:40:01.890
had access to before.

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:04.440
One last thing, Meera.

00:40:04.440 --> 00:40:06.930
If we took away the filters
that we just put there,

00:40:06.930 --> 00:40:10.140
and we just said, let's look
across the entire 7.7 million

00:40:10.140 --> 00:40:12.300
and we said, who's
at 90 and above--

00:40:12.300 --> 00:40:15.450
very high risk of addiction
for whatever reason--

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:19.507
it comes out to about
18,000 individuals.

00:40:19.507 --> 00:40:21.090
And now if you look
down at the bottom

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:23.620
there, it's a little hard
to see again on this screen,

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:27.490
but when we aggregate what are
those barriers to treatment?

00:40:27.490 --> 00:40:29.670
What you find is
they're about 10%.

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:32.310
Again, this is with data
from the last 30 days.

00:40:32.310 --> 00:40:36.630
About 10% would benefit
from a HUD housing credit,

00:40:36.630 --> 00:40:39.270
because their main
barrier to treatment

00:40:39.270 --> 00:40:41.160
is that they have
housing insecurity.

00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:44.788
And about 40% would benefit
from Medicaid expansion.

00:40:44.788 --> 00:40:46.080
They just don't have insurance.

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:48.660
But otherwise, there seems
to be reasonable stability

00:40:48.660 --> 00:40:51.310
in their life.

00:40:51.310 --> 00:40:53.310
And those barriers
to treatment, that's

00:40:53.310 --> 00:40:55.950
where a lot of the action is in
the marketplace to study that.

00:40:55.950 --> 00:40:58.320
But these are the kinds
of tools that the people

00:40:58.320 --> 00:41:01.590
on the front line can be given
to start studying that problem

00:41:01.590 --> 00:41:03.990
and see what works and
what doesn't, given

00:41:03.990 --> 00:41:05.030
a particular barrier.

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:06.990
MEERA KANHOUWA: So you
know there's actually

00:41:06.990 --> 00:41:12.690
peer-reviewed data that shows
that transportation is actually

00:41:12.690 --> 00:41:13.770
a barrier to treatment.

00:41:13.770 --> 00:41:18.210
And in fact, Dr. Harold Thomas,
who's a surgeon in the Bronx,

00:41:18.210 --> 00:41:22.110
did a study on breast cancer
individuals over a 10-year

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:26.490
period, and started the
Patient Navigation Institute

00:41:26.490 --> 00:41:29.400
as a result of that, and
was able to successfully

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:32.790
demonstrate that
there is a 60%--

00:41:32.790 --> 00:41:37.500
six zero percent-- increase
in morbidity and mortality

00:41:37.500 --> 00:41:41.550
when something as simple
as I don't have a ride

00:41:41.550 --> 00:41:44.010
to get to the doctor.

00:41:44.010 --> 00:41:46.950
So what's really interesting
as a result of this

00:41:46.950 --> 00:41:51.210
is that Medicare
has now approved

00:41:51.210 --> 00:41:56.520
Lyft and Uber for patients
to get to their appointments.

00:41:56.520 --> 00:42:00.570
Anthem, Cigna, Humana
are all allowing

00:42:00.570 --> 00:42:03.690
for people who fit
a certain category

00:42:03.690 --> 00:42:08.040
to be able to actually get
transportation to get there.

00:42:08.040 --> 00:42:09.780
And there is an
interesting study

00:42:09.780 --> 00:42:13.500
done about you're much
more likely to live

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:16.120
if you take an Uber to
the emergency department

00:42:16.120 --> 00:42:17.370
than if you take an ambulance.

00:42:17.370 --> 00:42:19.500
So just keep that in mind.

00:42:19.500 --> 00:42:22.140
If you want to go to
the ER, call an Uber,

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:24.360
unless you're having
a heart attack,

00:42:24.360 --> 00:42:26.450
then call your local ambulance.

00:42:26.450 --> 00:42:28.780
But it's pretty interesting.

00:42:28.780 --> 00:42:31.330
So transportation-- you
wouldn't think it's a big deal,

00:42:31.330 --> 00:42:33.002
but it is a pretty big deal.

00:42:33.002 --> 00:42:34.710
SEAN CONLIN: So that
example we just went

00:42:34.710 --> 00:42:37.140
through is more at
the policy level.

00:42:37.140 --> 00:42:40.520
A different persona for a user
for this kind of a capability

00:42:40.520 --> 00:42:42.790
would be more at the
caseworker level.

00:42:42.790 --> 00:42:45.750
Now we said all the
PII is protected.

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:49.050
And you're actually going to
see to the extent you can see it

00:42:49.050 --> 00:42:51.305
on the screen.

00:42:51.305 --> 00:42:52.430
Can you guys still hear me?

00:42:52.430 --> 00:42:53.370
I just hit my mic.

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:55.290
We're good?

00:42:55.290 --> 00:42:59.400
It's still genericized, but
at the caseworker level,

00:42:59.400 --> 00:43:04.260
if you think a Medicaid shop
or human services or child

00:43:04.260 --> 00:43:07.290
services using this
kind of capability too,

00:43:07.290 --> 00:43:09.660
that's more of a
caseworker use case

00:43:09.660 --> 00:43:11.340
where they've got
control of the data.

00:43:11.340 --> 00:43:13.170
They're using PII today.

00:43:13.170 --> 00:43:16.500
So they could use it just
on their limited data.

00:43:16.500 --> 00:43:18.000
And what you're
looking at here is--

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:21.790
the center nodes are patients.

00:43:21.790 --> 00:43:23.940
And it's a patient--

00:43:23.940 --> 00:43:26.670
we're looking at patients,
doctors, everybody

00:43:26.670 --> 00:43:30.390
in the ecosystem, the
entities, pharmacies, treatment

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:31.660
facilities.

00:43:31.660 --> 00:43:33.300
These are the high
risk ones in it,

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:35.590
somebody's area
of responsibility.

00:43:35.590 --> 00:43:38.470
So at a glance, you immediately
see where the risk factors are.

00:43:38.470 --> 00:43:41.130
The scoring is based on the
outputs of predictive AI

00:43:41.130 --> 00:43:42.960
models.

00:43:42.960 --> 00:43:46.500
And now this is a 360 view,
hence the name, of a patient,

00:43:46.500 --> 00:43:48.450
patient 400.

00:43:48.450 --> 00:43:49.650
It's real data.

00:43:49.650 --> 00:43:51.580
Well, it's patient 400.

00:43:51.580 --> 00:43:55.120
It has a red halo
around her or him.

00:43:55.120 --> 00:43:57.780
And that is indicative that the
predictive score says they're

00:43:57.780 --> 00:43:59.910
at high risk of addiction.

00:43:59.910 --> 00:44:02.255
They've got doctors
associated with them.

00:44:02.255 --> 00:44:03.630
They've got
lifestyle information

00:44:03.630 --> 00:44:05.370
associated with them.

00:44:05.370 --> 00:44:07.230
You can see them
appearing, and they're

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:10.140
receiving food stamps
and financial counseling.

00:44:10.140 --> 00:44:12.210
The power of this
is that we talked

00:44:12.210 --> 00:44:15.450
about health human services
and law enforcement data.

00:44:15.450 --> 00:44:17.440
When that starts
getting integrated,

00:44:17.440 --> 00:44:21.420
you can now do this network
analysis to see connections.

00:44:21.420 --> 00:44:23.610
I can see how much
is being spent

00:44:23.610 --> 00:44:25.980
on an individual
in the health area,

00:44:25.980 --> 00:44:30.990
but also their human services
area, and law enforcement,

00:44:30.990 --> 00:44:33.940
and not only them,
but their dependents.

00:44:33.940 --> 00:44:36.180
So we can start
connecting this data up.

00:44:36.180 --> 00:44:38.820
I said that we're using
AI to connect the data up.

00:44:38.820 --> 00:44:41.620
You could never, never
connect this kind of data

00:44:41.620 --> 00:44:43.290
up if you had to do it manually.

00:44:43.290 --> 00:44:45.000
But using some machine
learning models

00:44:45.000 --> 00:44:46.230
was one of the big
breakthroughs we've

00:44:46.230 --> 00:44:47.640
had here in the last two years.

00:44:47.640 --> 00:44:49.980
We're doing this at scale
both at the federal level

00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:51.750
and now down at the state level.

00:44:51.750 --> 00:44:55.620
And that connectedness is key
to being able to drive and feed

00:44:55.620 --> 00:44:56.997
the machine learning models.

00:44:56.997 --> 00:44:59.580
MEERA KANHOUWA: I would love to
have had this in the emergency

00:44:59.580 --> 00:45:00.190
department.

00:45:00.190 --> 00:45:03.260
We had a patient, we'll
call her Patient X,

00:45:03.260 --> 00:45:05.220
who used to come to our ED.

00:45:05.220 --> 00:45:07.710
And it took us about 18
months to figure out she

00:45:07.710 --> 00:45:09.480
was actually four
different people.

00:45:09.480 --> 00:45:12.390
And she would come in dressed
as four different people,

00:45:12.390 --> 00:45:14.850
different wigs, different
clothing styles.

00:45:14.850 --> 00:45:19.110
And she would get prescriptions
for percs, Demerol, T3s, et

00:45:19.110 --> 00:45:20.970
cetera, and sell them.

00:45:20.970 --> 00:45:22.230
And she'd sell the scripts.

00:45:22.230 --> 00:45:24.900
And she was using the money
to take care of her kids.

00:45:24.900 --> 00:45:28.700
Her kids hadn't been
signed up to CHIP

00:45:28.700 --> 00:45:30.420
and so they weren't
getting any benefits.

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:33.730
And so this was how
she was earning money.

00:45:33.730 --> 00:45:37.380
And it really took our ED
docs kind of saying you know,

00:45:37.380 --> 00:45:42.030
she looks really similar to that
girl I saw just a few days ago.

00:45:42.030 --> 00:45:45.570
But it took us about 18
months to connect the dots

00:45:45.570 --> 00:45:48.720
that she was the same person.

00:45:48.720 --> 00:45:51.420
And then we had to
track her effectively,

00:45:51.420 --> 00:45:53.490
to say look she might
have different hair,

00:45:53.490 --> 00:45:55.200
she might have
different clothes.

00:45:55.200 --> 00:45:57.700
But had we had
something like this,

00:45:57.700 --> 00:45:59.440
we wouldn't have had
to spend 18 months.

00:45:59.440 --> 00:46:03.150
Ultimately, we were able to get
her associated with the care

00:46:03.150 --> 00:46:06.000
team that she needed and
with the case management

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:08.010
that she needed.

00:46:08.010 --> 00:46:10.787
And I think that benefited
her and her family greatly.

00:46:10.787 --> 00:46:12.870
SEAN CONLIN: And part of
the tragedy of that story

00:46:12.870 --> 00:46:15.870
